course: biology
lesson: 158
title: Amazon giants and tall tales: anacondas, caimans, electric eels and the candiru
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Amazon has real giants: the green anaconda, the heaviest snake on Earth, lies in a swamp with only its eyes and nostrils above the water, and the black caiman can grow more than four metres long.
explain: Around such animals tall tales grow fast: explorers told of anacondas thirty metres long, and travellers of a tiny fish that swims up into swimmers.
explain: A scientist checks a tale by measuring and testing: no anaconda over about seven metres has ever been well measured, and the candiru story rests on a few unclear reports.
explain: Cartoons show snakes crushing bones like a garden hose tied in knots; a real anaconda squeezes until the prey's blood stops flowing, and it is a shy animal that mostly avoids people.
fact: The green anaconda is the heaviest snake in the world; water holds its weight up, and its eyes and nostrils sit on top of its head.
fact: An anaconda kills by squeezing until the prey's blood stops flowing, not by crushing bones.
fact: The longest well-measured anacondas are about seven metres; stories of twenty or thirty metres were never proved, and dried skins stretch and look longer than the snake was.
fact: The black caiman is the biggest predator of the Amazon, over four metres long, and hunts fish and capybaras at night.
fact: When hunters killed most black caimans, piranhas grew in number, because caimans had been eating them.
fact: Electric eels can leap up and press their chin against a threat, so the shock goes straight into it; the scientist Kenneth Catania tested this and wrote about it in 2016 and 2017.
fact: The candiru is a tiny, thin catfish that feeds on the blood of bigger fish by entering their gills; the story of it attacking people rests on very few unclear reports, and scientists doubt most of them.
fact: Caimans rarely attack big grown capybaras, which are too big and alert, and the two often share river banks.
fact: The capybara is the biggest rodent alive and can weigh as much as a grown man.
fact: The horned frog has a mouth nearly as wide as its body, grabs anything that fits, even mice, and is nicknamed the Pac-Man frog.
fact: Some harmless snakes copy the red, black and yellow bands of the deadly coral snake, so predators avoid both, and it is not safe to guess which is which.
fact: People cut and burn parts of the Amazon for farms and ranches, and because trees help make the Amazon's rain, when trees go, less rain falls, soil washes away and animals lose homes.
fact: The giant armadillo can dig a big burrow in a night, and lizards, ocelots (small wild cats) and birds later shelter in it.
fact: Caimans' eyes glow red-orange in torchlight because a shiny layer behind the eye reflects light, and guides count caimans by pairs of glowing eyes.
exercise: What is the green anaconda famous for? | Being the heaviest snake in the world | Being the most poisonous snake | Being the fastest snake | Being able to glide between the trees by flattening its body
exercise: Why does an anaconda's head have eyes and nostrils on top? | So it can see and breathe while almost hidden under water | So it can see birds above | So rain cannot get in its mouth | So it can sleep upside down while it hangs by its tail from a branch
exercise: How does an anaconda kill its prey? | It squeezes until the blood stops flowing | It crushes every single bone in the animal into tiny pieces | It bites with deadly poison | It drowns it by sitting on it
exercise: How long are the longest well-measured anacondas? | About seven metres | About seventy centimetres | About thirty metres | About one hundred metres
exercise: Why can an old snake skin look longer than the snake was? | Skins stretch when they dry | Snakes keep growing after they die | Skins are always sewn together | The skin keeps growing on its own
exercise: How long can a black caiman grow? | More than four metres | About forty centimetres | Only about one metre long | About fourteen metres long
exercise: What happened when hunters killed most black caimans? | Piranhas grew in number | Piranhas died out | The river dried up | Capybaras disappeared
exercise: Why does an electric eel leap and press its chin on an attacker? | The shock goes straight into the attacker instead of the water | To bite it with sharp teeth | To get a breath of air | To give it a friendly hug, as eels often do with other animals they meet
exercise: What does the candiru really feed on? | Blood of bigger fish, through their gills | Swimmers in every river | Leaves, fruit and seeds that fall into the water | Mosquito larvae only
exercise: What do scientists say about the candiru story? | It rests on very few unclear reports and they doubt most of it | It happens to swimmers every day | It was proved in a big experiment with hundreds of swimmers in the river | It is true only in winter
exercise: Why do caimans rarely attack grown capybaras? | A grown capybara is too big and alert | Capybaras have a poisonous, bad-tasting skin | Caimans only eat plants, so they leave all animals alone | Capybaras live up in trees, out of reach
exercise: What is a capybara? | The biggest rodent alive | A kind of small pig | A baby tapir | A kind of monkey
exercise: Why is the horned frog nicknamed the Pac-Man frog? | It is round with a huge mouth like the game character | It eats little dots | It glows yellow at night, so it lights up the forest floor | It runs through mazes
exercise: Why do some harmless snakes have red, black and yellow bands? | They copy the deadly coral snake so predators avoid them | The colours keep them warm | They are ill | They want to be seen by friends
exercise: What happens when forest is cut and burned? | Less rain falls, soil washes away and animals lose homes | More rain falls and the soil gets richer from all the burnt wood | Animals get more space and food | Nothing changes for animals
exercise: Which animals use a giant armadillo's old burrow? | Lizards, ocelots and birds | Only other armadillos | Fish | No other animal at all, because the holes are too small
exercise: Why do caimans' eyes glow red in torchlight? | A shiny layer behind the eye reflects the light | Their eyes make their own fire | They are angry | They have tiny lights inside their heads that switch on at night
test: An old story tells of a hunter who found an anaconda as long as a bus, about 12 metres. What would a scientist say? | No anaconda over about seven metres has been well measured, so the tale is doubtful | Totally true, they are often that long | Too short, because they are often found reaching a hundred metres in the jungle | Snakes cannot grow longer than a metre
test: In a cartoon a snake wraps a hero and you hear his bones crack like twigs. What is true for anacondas? | They squeeze until blood stops flowing, not by cracking bones | Exactly what anacondas do | Anacondas never squeeze; they swallow animals while they are still kicking | Anacondas only bite
test: «they say a tiny fish swims up into you if you wee in the Amazon» What is right? | The candiru story rests on very few unclear reports, and scientists doubt most of them | It is proved and happens daily | It only happens to adults | The fish is as big as a dolphin and chases people who swim in the river every day
test: A guide's torch shows 7 pairs of glowing red eyes, sorry, 8 pairs. How many caimans probably? | 8 | 7 | 16 | 14
test: A capybara family of 5 grazes while one caiman lies on the same bank. How many legs on the bank altogether? | 24 | 20 | 28 | 22
test: What would happen if the black caimans came back to a river where they had been hunted? | Piranha numbers would go down again | Piranhas would all grow huge and fat | All the other fish would vanish at once | Capybaras would come back and start eating the fish
test: «red touches yellow, that snake's harmless, I'll pick it up» What is the best answer? | Don't; harmless snakes copy coral snakes, and you cannot be sure, so leave it | Fine, pick it up | Fine, if you are quick | Fine, because coral snakes are harmless and only look scary to keep birds away
test: An electric eel leaps out at a hand. Why jump instead of staying in the water? | Out of water the shock goes straight into the hand instead of spreading out | It is trying to fly | It needs to dry its skin | It wants to bite the hand, because electric eels attack anything that lifts them out of the water
test: «why's that big frog eating a mouse, frogs eat flies» What is right? | The horned frog grabs anything that fits in its huge mouth, even mice | That is not a frog | Frogs only ever eat flies and worms, so the picture must be a trick made with a computer | It is just holding the mouse
test: Smoke rises where forest is being cleared. Which is a likely result? | Less rain, because the trees help make the Amazon's rain | More rain would fall everywhere, because the open ground would be cooler | Animals get bigger homes | The river becomes deeper
test: A giant armadillo moves out of its burrow. Who might move in? | A lizard, an ocelot or birds | A fish washed in from a nearby stream | A young caiman from the river | Nobody, burrows collapse at once
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the maximum length of the green anaconda. | No specimen exceeding approximately seven metres has been reliably measured | Specimens of thirty metres are frequently recorded | The species does not exceed one metre in length | The length of the species has never been measured at all, so no figure can be given
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the electric eel may leap from the water against a threatening animal. | The discharge thereby passes directly into the animal rather than dispersing through the water | The eel thereby obtains a breath of air | The eel thereby bites the animal with its teeth and holds it fast until it ceases entirely to struggle | The eel thereby escapes into the forest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reports of the candiru attacking human beings? | They rest upon very few unclear reports, most of which scientists regard with doubt | They are confirmed by numerous experiments | They concern a fish of the dimensions of a dolphin | They are reported exclusively in respect of adults bathing in the sea
exam: Ascertain the consequence observed upon the killing by hunters of the majority of black caimans. | An increase in the number of piranhas, upon which the caimans had preyed | A decline in the number of piranhas | The disappearance of capybaras from the river, the caimans having driven them away | The drying of the river channel
